number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|A broadly enrollable template can assert a privileged Entra CBA identity|An unapproved enrollee can choose an authentication identity on a published template without effective approval restrictions AND the issuing chain and mapping are accepted by Entra CBA for a privileged target.|Template and CA enrollment ACLs; name/EKU flags; approval/signature requirements; Entra CA trust; username binding and target role.|Restrict enrollment and subject control and enforce strong supported identity binding in CBA.|AD certificate authentication does not automatically authenticate to Entra. CBA trust, mapping, and authentication strength are mandatory prerequisites.|T1649;T1078.004|AC-3;IA-5;SC-17|Protect certificate issuance and the identity binding accepted by the cloud.||High
002|An enrollment-agent path reaches an AD identity that administers a hybrid bridge|An unapproved principal can obtain and use an enrollment-agent certificate for a target identity AND that identity has effective administration of a hybrid bridge.|Agent template issuance; enrollment-agent restrictions; target template and CA ACL; strong mapping; bridge administrator path.|Restrict enrollment-agent scope and separate certificate administration from bridge administration.|An enrollment-agent EKU alone is not sufficient. Issuance approval and agent restrictions may block the requested identity.|T1649;T1078.002|AC-3;AC-6;SC-17|Constrain delegated certificate issuance for control-plane identities.||High
003|Template write access can create an authentication path to a bridge administrator|An unapproved principal can effectively change a published authentication template AND the change can satisfy enrollment and mapping prerequisites for an identity that administers a hybrid asset.|Template owner/DACL; relevant writable properties; publication state; CA constraints; identity mapping; target authorization.|Remove unintended template-write authority and review published authentication templates.|Template control is a candidate path until CA policy, enrollment permission, and current strong-mapping behavior are resolved.|T1649|AC-3;CM-5;SC-17|Protect configuration changes that determine authentication-certificate issuance.||High
004|CA management authority crosses into privileged cloud authentication|An unapproved CA manager or certificate officer can perform supported issuance/approval changes that yield an accepted certificate for a privileged Entra CBA user.|CA role separation; effective permissions; issuance restrictions; chain trust; CBA mapping; privileged target.|Restrict CA administrative roles and protect the cloud's accepted certificate trust.|ManageCA and ManageCertificates are not interchangeable. Do not assume either permission alone grants arbitrary valid certificates.|T1649;T1078.004|AC-5;AC-6;SC-17|Separate certificate duties and constrain authority accepted by cloud authentication.||High
005|An exposed CA host can use a key trusted for cloud authentication|A lower-trust administrator controls a CA host AND an effective key-use path can sign certificates in a chain trusted by Entra CBA for sensitive identities.|Host administration; CA key provider and ACL metadata; HSM/use boundary; trusted chain; user-binding policy.|Protect CA hosts and key-use authorization to the level of the identities they can certify.|Host administration does not prove access to an independently administered HSM key. Public CA certificates are not sensitive key evidence.|T1649|AC-6;SC-12;SC-17|Protect CA signing authority and the cloud trust derived from it.||High
006|An exposed enrollment endpoint completes a relay path to a hybrid administrator|A supported certificate enrollment endpoint accepts relayable authentication without the required protection AND a reachable authentication source and issuance/mapping path can yield a hybrid administrator identity.|Endpoint type; effective EPA/TLS/RPC protection; authentication source/reachability evidence; template issuance; current mapping; target rights.|Harden the enrollment endpoint and remove unnecessary relay and issuance paths.|No coercion or relay tests. Missing one endpoint setting alone does not prove a working certificate relay path.|T1557;T1649|IA-2;SC-8;SC-17|Protect enrollment authentication and bind the issued certificate to the correct identity.||High
007|Effective weak certificate mapping reaches a hybrid control-plane identity|Current DC patch and effective mapping behavior permit a weak mapping for a controllable certificate identity AND the resulting AD identity administers a hybrid asset.|DC OS/update state; effective certificate mapping; template constraints; mapping attributes; target privileges.|Apply supported strong-mapping requirements and remove unsafe alternate identity mappings.|Do not report legacy compatibility registry values as effective on patched systems that no longer honor them.|T1649|IA-4;IA-5;SC-17|Maintain trustworthy certificate-to-account binding for privileged identities.||High
008|Revocation controls do not meet policy for a cloud-trusted issuing chain|A CA chain trusted for sensitive Entra CBA identities lacks the required current revocation publication or supported relying-party configuration according to the organization's defined requirement.|Trusted chain; CBA revocation configuration; approved publication checks or supplied evidence; issuance scope; requirement and review date.|Restore supported revocation publication and verify relying-party enforcement with an approved test plan.|A failed HTTP fetch does not prove authentication succeeds with a revoked certificate. Report the control gap, not a bypass.|T1649|IA-5;SC-17|Manage certificate validity and revocation for trusted authenticators.||Medium
